IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `gva-dates`
IMPORT `gva-core`

-- Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) -- the questions people ask, for three seats in one layer.
--
-- SEATS: the person who put graffiti on property, or is accused of it; the person who sells spray paint or wide
-- markers; the owner or occupier of property with graffiti on it. The seat is a parameter: every exported question
-- takes `my role` first ("Which of these describes you"). A question about another role answers `does not arise for
-- you` without reading any other input, so the form never asks those inputs; a question whose answer is fixed for its
-- role settles on the role alone, so the form can drop it. The local government is never a seat; its duties and
-- powers appear only as what they mean for the person asking.
--
-- SITUATIONS (design D8): the situation is a parameter too. Within a role, a question that arises only in a situation
-- takes that situation's fact straight after `my role`, and when the situation does not hold it answers `does not
-- arise for you` without reading any other input, exactly as for the role. The questions of one situation share one
-- input (the same name, type and @desc), so the form asks it once and one answer settles them all. The situations are:
-- for the owner or occupier, graffiti put up with the consent of the owner or occupier (ss 18-24) or without it
-- (ss 25, 5); a notice under s 18 given (ss 18-24, after consent); an objection or review lodged (s 24, after the
-- notice); the local government wanting to come on to the land (ss 27-30); graffiti removal work on the property
-- (s 12); and for the person accused, what they are accused of (s 5, s 6 or both). Every seller's question arises for
-- every seller, so the seller has no situation input; nor does a question that arises in every situation of its role.
--
-- The rules are in gva-core.l4. This module builds the core's inputs from plain answers and restates no rule. Each
-- regulative rule a role meets is projected four ways: does it apply to me, by when, have I complied (the rule run over
-- what happened, inside the question), and what follows if not. No exported question takes a DEONTIC or EVENT input;
-- the rules and events are built here and stay inside L4.
--
-- Deadlines are the Act's own, counted under the Interpretation Act 1984 s 61(1)(b) (the first day left out). The last
-- day to object (s 22(2)) or to apply for review (s 23(3)), if it falls on a Saturday, Sunday or public holiday, moves
-- to the next day that is not one (s 61(1)(e)). The public holidays counted are the Public and Bank Holidays Act 1972
-- Second Schedule's, apart from the King's Birthday and other days appointed by proclamation, which nothing deposited
-- fixes; the question texts say so. The time a s 18 notice sets is the notice's own and is not moved (NOTES.md
-- finding 11).
--
-- BASELINE. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) -- the questions people ask`

§§ `Who is asking, and the shape of the answers`

DECLARE Role IS ONE OF
    `I put graffiti on property, or am accused of it`
    `I sell spray paint or wide markers`
    `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it`

-- The accused's situation (D8): what they did, or are accused of. The s 5 questions, with ss 8 and 10, which follow
-- only a s 5 conviction ("offender", s 4), arise for s 5 or both; the s 6 questions for s 6 or both. s 13 forfeiture
-- follows a conviction for any offence under the Act, so it arises for every value and does not take this input.
DECLARE `What I am accused of` IS ONE OF
    `damaging property by graffiti (s 5)`
    `having a thing with me to use for graffiti (s 6)`
    `both`

DECLARE `Yes or no` IS ONE OF
    yes
    no
    `does not arise for you`

DECLARE `Amount` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    amount HAS dollars IS A NUMBER

DECLARE `Years` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `imprisonment for` HAS `years` IS A NUMBER

DECLARE `Hours` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `at least` HAS `hours` IS A NUMBER

DECLARE `Date answer` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `by` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `between` HAS `the first day` IS A DATE
                  `the last day` IS A DATE

DECLARE `Done in time or not` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `done in time`
    `not done in time`
    `still open until` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `done too early, before the right to do it arose on` HAS `the first day` IS A DATE
    `not open yet: it opens on` HAS `the first day` IS A DATE
                                    `the last day` IS A DATE

DECLARE `Least penalty` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `at least a community based order (Sentencing Act 1995)`
    `at least a youth community based order (Young Offenders Act 1994)`

-- Where no notice under s 18 has been given, the s 19 question does not arise (D8), so it has no answer of its own
-- for that case.
DECLARE `What the local government may do` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `nothing: the graffiti has been obliterated`
    `nothing yet: the time in the notice has not run out`
    `nothing for now: the notice is suspended until the objection or review is decided (s 24)`
    `obliterate the graffiti, and recover its reasonable cost from you as a debt`
    `obliterate the graffiti, and recover its reasonable cost from the person given the notice as a debt`

DECLARE `Who may be ordered to pay me back` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `the occupier, as much as the court considers fair and reasonable`
    `the owner, as much as the court considers fair and reasonable`
    `no one: you both own and occupy the property`
    `no one: you have had no expense, fine or cost of the kind in s 19(5)`
    `no one: the notice was not given to you`

DECLARE `Whether I can be sued` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `no: as the owner, an action in tort does not lie against you; it may be taken against the State instead`
    `yes: your breach of your duty of care would amount to gross negligence, which s 12 does not protect`
    `s 12 does not protect you: it protects only the owner of the place, not an occupier who is not its owner`

-- The owner's or occupier's one fact about a notice under s 18: who it went to, and how they stand to the property.
-- It answers s 18(3) (the owner told), s 19(5)-(6) (who pays back), s 20 (affected person) and s 18(5) (who is fined).
-- It is also the notice situation (D8): its last value, no notice, settles every question of ss 18-24 but the first to
-- `does not arise for you`.
DECLARE `The notice and me` IS ONE OF
    `it was given to me, and I own the property`
    `it was given to me, and I occupy the property but do not own it`
    `it was given to me, and I both own and occupy the property`
    `it was given to the occupier, and I own the property`
    `it was given to the owner, and I occupy the property but do not own it`
    `no notice has been given to me, or to the owner or occupier of my property`

§§ `Building the core's inputs`

GIVEN `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the notice was given to me` IF
    CONSIDER `the notice and me`
    WHEN `it was given to me, and I own the property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `it was given to me, and I occupy the property but do not own it` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `it was given to me, and I both own and occupy the property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `it was given to the occupier, and I own the property` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `it was given to the owner, and I occupy the property but do not own it` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `no notice has been given to me, or to the owner or occupier of my property` THEN FALSE

-- s 20's question, from the seat: was the notice given to me, or to my occupier while I own the property?
GIVEN `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me`
GIVETH A `Who the notice was given to`
`the s 20 position of` `the notice and me` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the notice and me`
    WHEN `it was given to me, and I own the property` THEN `to the person`
    WHEN `it was given to me, and I occupy the property but do not own it` THEN `to the person`
    WHEN `it was given to me, and I both own and occupy the property` THEN `to the person`
    WHEN `it was given to the occupier, and I own the property` THEN `to the occupier of the person's property, and the person is its owner`
    WHEN `it was given to the owner, and I occupy the property but do not own it` THEN `to someone else, or no notice has been given`
    WHEN `no notice has been given to me, or to the owner or occupier of my property` THEN `to someone else, or no notice has been given`

-- How the person given the notice stands to the property (s 18(3), s 19(6)), or NOTHING if no notice was given.
GIVEN `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Owner or occupier`
`the person given the notice is` `the notice and me` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the notice and me`
    WHEN `it was given to me, and I own the property` THEN JUST `the owner`
    WHEN `it was given to me, and I occupy the property but do not own it` THEN JUST `the occupier, and not the owner`
    WHEN `it was given to me, and I both own and occupy the property` THEN JUST `both the owner and the occupier`
    WHEN `it was given to the occupier, and I own the property` THEN JUST `the occupier, and not the owner`
    WHEN `it was given to the owner, and I occupy the property but do not own it` THEN JUST `the owner`
    WHEN `no notice has been given to me, or to the owner or occupier of my property` THEN NOTHING

GIVEN `it holds` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`yes if` `it holds` MEANS
    IF `it holds` THEN yes ELSE no

§§ `The situations a question arises in (design D8)`

-- The notice situation of ss 18-24, read in order: graffiti put up with the consent of the owner or occupier
-- (s 18(1)(a); graffiti without it is a s 25 matter), and then a notice under s 18(2) given to me, or to the owner or
-- occupier of my property. Where there was no consent the notice is not read.
GIVEN `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the notice situation does not hold` IF
    IF `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier`
    THEN CONSIDER `the notice and me`
         WHEN `it was given to me, and I own the property` THEN FALSE
         WHEN `it was given to me, and I occupy the property but do not own it` THEN FALSE
         WHEN `it was given to me, and I both own and occupy the property` THEN FALSE
         WHEN `it was given to the occupier, and I own the property` THEN FALSE
         WHEN `it was given to the owner, and I occupy the property but do not own it` THEN FALSE
         WHEN `no notice has been given to me, or to the owner or occupier of my property` THEN TRUE
    ELSE TRUE

-- The accused's situation: the s 5 questions do not arise for someone accused only under s 6, and the s 6 questions
-- not for someone accused only under s 5.
--
-- These two helpers, and the four above that read `the notice and me`, list every value and have no OTHERWISE. On the
-- stable l4 a CONSIDER with OTHERWISE takes the OTHERWISE branch for an ASSUMEd value instead of failing (NOTES.md
-- finding 31), so a read of an ASSUMEd notice or accusation in the cases would go unnoticed; without OTHERWISE it fails.
GIVEN `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 5 situation does not hold` IF
    CONSIDER `what I am accused of`
    WHEN `damaging property by graffiti (s 5)` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `having a thing with me to use for graffiti (s 6)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `both` THEN FALSE

GIVEN `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 6 situation does not hold` IF
    CONSIDER `what I am accused of`
    WHEN `damaging property by graffiti (s 5)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `having a thing with me to use for graffiti (s 6)` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `both` THEN FALSE

§§ `Running a regulative rule over what happened`

-- Not a provision of the Act: the one helper this layer uses to ask a regulative rule of the core what became of it.
-- It runs the rule from its start against the act, done by the party at the time given (a day number, or a minute
-- for the entry rule), and reports the outcome.
--
-- On the stable l4 (1.6.98) a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH ("WHEN BREACH" is a parse
-- error), and OTHERWISE takes a breach and a rule still waiting alike (NOTES.md finding 20). So where the act has been
-- done, a FULFILLED trace means it was done in time, and anything else that it was not. Where the act has not been
-- done, the helper asks whether doing it now would keep the rule: if so the rule is still open, and if not its time has
-- run out. The WAITUNTIL before each event makes a deadline that has passed take effect at that moment, so that a rule
-- under HENCE (the entry permission after 24 hours) can be matched by the event.
--
-- One case the rule cannot tell: an act dated before the rule starts counts as in time (NOTES.md finding 23). So, as
-- design D5 says for what CONSIDER cannot tell apart, each question checks that case first from the dates (an
-- objection before the notice, an application before the right arose) and does not ask the rule.
--
-- It takes a DEONTIC value, so it is never exported; the exported questions take plain facts and call it.
DECLARE `Outcome of a rule` IS ONE OF
    `the rule was kept`
    `the rule was not kept`
    `the rule is still open`

GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
      `the party` IS A `Graffiti party`
      `the act` IS A `Graffiti act`
      `the time the rule starts` IS A NUMBER
      `the act has been done` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the time the act was done` IS A NUMBER
      `the time now` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a rule`
`the outcome of` MEANS
    IF `the act has been done`
    THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` `the time the rule starts` (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the time the act was done`), (EVENT `the party` `the act` `the time the act was done`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the rule was kept`
         OTHERWISE `the rule was not kept`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` `the time the rule starts` (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the time now`), (EVENT `the party` `the act` `the time now`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the rule is still open`
         OTHERWISE `the rule was not kept`

GIVEN `the outcome` IS AN `Outcome of a rule`, `the last day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Done in time or not`
`in time or not, from` `the outcome` `the last day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the outcome`
    WHEN `the rule was kept` THEN `done in time`
    WHEN `the rule was not kept` THEN `not done in time`
    WHEN `the rule is still open` THEN `still open until` `the last day`

-- A prohibition run over the act: a breach means the act was an offence.
GIVEN `the outcome` IS AN `Outcome of a rule`
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`yes if it was broken` `the outcome` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the outcome`
    WHEN `the rule was not kept` THEN yes
    OTHERWISE no

§§ `For the person who put graffiti on property, or is accused of it (ss 5, 6, 8, 10, 13)`

@export Is it an offence for me to put this mark on this property, a tree or other plant included (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) ss 3, 5)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of` @desc What you did, or are accused of, under the Act
      `how the mark was applied` IS A `How the mark was applied` @desc How the mark was, or would be, put on the property: by spraying, writing, drawing, marking or otherwise applying paint or another marking substance; by scratching or etching; or in some other way, such as sticking on a poster or sticker (not graffiti under s 3)
      `the property belongs to someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the property belongs to someone other than you: a wall, fence, vehicle, sign, or a tree, hedge or other plant (vegetation is property here, s 5(2))
      `they consented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person the property belongs to consented to the mark being put on it
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is it an offence for me to put this mark on this property` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I put graffiti on property, or am accused of it` THEN
        IF `the s 5 situation does not hold` `what I am accused of`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `yes if it was broken` (`the outcome of` (`s 5(1) -- a person must not damage another person's property by graffiti without consent` `how the mark was applied` `the property belongs to someone else` `they consented`) `the person` `put the mark on the property` 0 TRUE 0 0)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export What is the most I can be fined for damaging property by graffiti (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 5(1))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of` @desc What you did, or are accused of, under the Act
GIVETH AN `Amount`
`What is the most I can be fined for damaging property by graffiti` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I put graffiti on property, or am accused of it` THEN
        IF `the s 5 situation does not hold` `what I am accused of`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE amount `s 5(1) -- the maximum fine`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned for damaging property by graffiti (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 5(1); the fine and imprisonment may be imposed together or either alone)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of` @desc What you did, or are accused of, under the Act
GIVETH A `Years`
`What is the longest I can be imprisoned for damaging property by graffiti` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I put graffiti on property, or am accused of it` THEN
        IF `the s 5 situation does not hold` `what I am accused of`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `imprisonment for` `s 5(1) -- the maximum imprisonment, in years`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export If I am convicted of damaging property by graffiti, what is the least penalty the court must give me (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 5(1)(a), (b))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of` @desc What you did, or are accused of, under the Act
      `my age when it happened` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in years when the graffiti was put on the property (an adult is 18 or over, s 4)
GIVETH A `Least penalty`
`What is the least penalty I can be given for damaging property by graffiti` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I put graffiti on property, or am accused of it` THEN
        IF `the s 5 situation does not hold` `what I am accused of`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE CONSIDER `s 5(1)(a), (b) -- the minimum penalty` `my age when it happened`
             WHEN `a community based order under the Sentencing Act 1995` THEN `at least a community based order (Sentencing Act 1995)`
             WHEN `a youth community based order under the Young Offenders Act 1994` THEN `at least a youth community based order (Young Offenders Act 1994)`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export If I am sentenced to a community order for damaging property by graffiti, how many hours of unpaid community work must it require, at the least, as graffiti removal where practicable (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 10)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of` @desc What you did, or are accused of, under the Act
GIVETH A `Hours`
`How many hours of unpaid community work must my community order require` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I put graffiti on property, or am accused of it` THEN
        IF `the s 5 situation does not hold` `what I am accused of`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `at least` `s 10(1) -- the minimum hours of unpaid community work in a community order`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export If I am convicted of damaging property by graffiti, can the court order me to restore the property, or to pay someone else to restore it (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 8)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of` @desc What you did, or are accused of, under the Act
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Can the court order me to restore the property or pay for its restoration` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    -- Asked of a conviction under s 5, so the s 8(2) fact is TRUE by the question's own terms.
    WHEN `I put graffiti on property, or am accused of it` THEN
        IF `the s 5 situation does not hold` `what I am accused of`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `yes if` (`s 8(2) -- the court may order the offender to restore the property, or to pay another person to` TRUE)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

-- s 13 follows a conviction for any offence under the Act, so it arises whatever the accused is accused of, and takes
-- no situation input (D8).
@export If I am convicted of an offence under the Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA), can the court order this thing of mine forfeited, such as my spray cans or the phone I filmed the graffiti with (s 13)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the thing` IS A `Thing that may be forfeited` @desc What the thing is: a graffiti implement (a can of spray paint, or a pen or marker with a tip over 6 mm and permanent ink); a thing you used to record, store or send images of the graffiti you caused, such as a phone or camera; another thing used, or meant to be used, in or in connection with the offence; or a thing not connected with the offence
      `the prosecutor has applied for it to be forfeited` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the prosecutor has applied to the court for the thing to be forfeited (without that application the court cannot order it, s 13(4); you must also have the chance to show cause, Criminal Code s 731(2))
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Can the court order this thing of mine forfeited` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I put graffiti on property, or am accused of it` THEN `yes if` (`s 13 -- the court may order the thing forfeited on conviction` `the thing` `the prosecutor has applied for it to be forfeited`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export Is it an offence for me to have this thing with me, given that the law can presume I meant to use it for graffiti (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 6)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of` @desc What you did, or are accused of, under the Act
      `I intend to use it to put graffiti on property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have the thing (spray paint, a marker, anything else) with the intention of using it to put graffiti on property; having it includes having it under your control while someone else holds it (s 4)
      `the circumstances would give rise to a reasonable suspicion that I intend that` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the circumstances in which you have it would give rise to a reasonable suspicion that you intend to use it for graffiti (a court's judgment; if so, the intention is presumed, s 6(2))
      `I can prove that I did not intend that` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you could prove to the court that you did not have that intention (for the court to decide)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is it an offence for me to have this thing with me` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I put graffiti on property, or am accused of it` THEN
        IF `the s 6 situation does not hold` `what I am accused of`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `yes if it was broken` (`the outcome of` (`s 6(1) -- a person must not possess a thing with the intention of applying graffiti with it` `I intend to use it to put graffiti on property` `the circumstances would give rise to a reasonable suspicion that I intend that` `I can prove that I did not intend that`) `the person` `be in possession of the thing` 0 TRUE 0 0)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export What is the most I can be fined for having a thing with the intention of using it for graffiti (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 6(1))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of` @desc What you did, or are accused of, under the Act
GIVETH AN `Amount`
`What is the most I can be fined for having a thing to use for graffiti` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I put graffiti on property, or am accused of it` THEN
        IF `the s 6 situation does not hold` `what I am accused of`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE amount `s 6(1) -- the maximum fine`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§ `For the person who sells spray paint or wide markers (ss 4, 7)`

-- Every question here arises for every seller, so none takes a situation input (D8).

@export Is this item a graffiti implement: a can of spray paint, or a pen or marker with a tip over 6 mm wide and ink that is not water soluble (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 4)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the kind of item` IS A `Kind of item` @desc What the item is: a can of spray paint; a pen or marker pen, or a similar implement; or something else
      `the width of its tip in millimetres` IS A NUMBER @desc For a pen or marker, the width of its tip in millimetres
      `its ink is not water soluble and can mark a surface` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a pen or marker, whether it contains a fluid that is not water soluble and is capable of marking a surface (permanent ink)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is this item a graffiti implement` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I sell spray paint or wide markers` THEN `yes if` (`s 4 -- the item is a graffiti implement` `the kind of item` `the width of its tip in millimetres` `its ink is not water soluble and can mark a surface`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export Is my sale of this item to this buyer an offence, allowing for the defence that I believed on reasonable grounds the buyer was an adult (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) ss 4, 7)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the kind of item` IS A `Kind of item` @desc What the item is: a can of spray paint; a pen or marker pen, or a similar implement; or something else
      `the width of its tip in millimetres` IS A NUMBER @desc For a pen or marker, the width of its tip in millimetres
      `its ink is not water soluble and can mark a surface` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a pen or marker, whether it contains a fluid that is not water soluble and is capable of marking a surface (permanent ink)
      `the buyer's age` IS A NUMBER @desc The buyer's age in years (a child is under 18, s 4)
      `I, or the person selling for me, believed the buyer was an adult` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, or the employee or other person selling on your behalf, believed the buyer was 18 or over
      `the belief was on reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court would accept that the belief was on reasonable grounds, for example because identification was checked (for the court to decide; you must prove it)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is my sale of this item to this buyer an offence` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I sell spray paint or wide markers` THEN
        `yes if it was broken` (`the outcome of` (`s 7 -- a person must not sell a graffiti implement to a child, unless they believed on reasonable grounds the buyer was an adult` `the kind of item` `the width of its tip in millimetres` `its ink is not water soluble and can mark a surface` `the buyer's age` `I, or the person selling for me, believed the buyer was an adult` `the belief was on reasonable grounds`) `the seller` `sell the item to the buyer` 0 TRUE 0 0)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export If I am charged with selling a graffiti implement to a child, do I have the defence that I, or the person selling for me, believed on reasonable grounds that the buyer was an adult (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 7(2))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `I, or the person selling for me, believed the buyer was an adult` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, or the employee or other person selling on your behalf, believed the buyer was 18 or over
      `the belief was on reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court would accept that the belief was on reasonable grounds, for example because identification was checked (for the court to decide; you must prove it)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Do I have the defence that I believed the buyer was an adult` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I sell spray paint or wide markers` THEN `yes if` (`s 7(2) -- the seller has a defence` `I, or the person selling for me, believed the buyer was an adult` `the belief was on reasonable grounds`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export What is the most I can be fined for selling a graffiti implement to a child: a first offence, or a later one (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 7(1)(a), (b))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `I have been convicted of selling a graffiti implement to a child before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already been convicted of an offence under s 7(1), so that this would be a subsequent offence
GIVETH AN `Amount`
`What is the most I can be fined for selling a graffiti implement to a child` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I sell spray paint or wide markers` THEN amount (`s 7(1) -- the maximum fine` `I have been convicted of selling a graffiti implement to a child before`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§ `For the owner or occupier of property with graffiti on it (Part 3, s 12)`

§§§ `s 18 -- a notice to remove graffiti`

-- This first question takes the consent fact second, as the questions of ss 18-24 do, but answers it rather than
-- dropping out: without consent the answer is no (s 18(1)(a) confines the notice to graffiti put up with consent). It
-- is the answer an owner whose wall was tagged wants: they cannot be made to remove it; the local government may
-- remove it itself (s 25). The other inputs are read only with consent (D8; NOTES.md).
@export Can the local government require me, by a written notice, to have this graffiti obliterated (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 18(1), (2))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `it is visible from a public place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it can be seen from a public place: anywhere the public may go, a school or other place of education (except parts closed to students and the public), or an unoccupied private place (s 14)
      `the local government considers it unsightly or offensive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government considers the graffiti unsightly or offensive (its judgment; a notice under s 18 means it does)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Can the local government require me to have the graffiti obliterated` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN `yes if` (`s 18(1) -- section 18 applies to the graffiti` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `it is visible from a public place` `the local government considers it unsightly or offensive`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

-- From here to s 24, each question arises only for graffiti put up with consent and once a notice under s 18 has been
-- given: it takes the consent fact second and the notice third, and answers `does not arise for you` without reading
-- anything else where either fails (D8).
@export Must I have the graffiti obliterated, in a manner the local government accepts, under a notice it gave me (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 18(1), (2), (4))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `it is visible from a public place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it can be seen from a public place: anywhere the public may go, a school or other place of education (except parts closed to students and the public), or an unoccupied private place (s 14)
      `the local government considers it unsightly or offensive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government considers the graffiti unsightly or offensive (its judgment; a notice under s 18 means it does)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Must I have the graffiti obliterated` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `yes if` (`s 18(1), (2) -- the person must ensure the graffiti is obliterated` (`the notice was given to me` `the notice and me`) `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `it is visible from a public place` `the local government considers it unsightly or offensive`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export By what date must the graffiti be obliterated under the local government's notice (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 18(2); the date is the notice's own time, not moved off a weekend or public holiday; while an objection or review is undecided the notice may be suspended, s 24, and how its time runs afterwards is not settled)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave the notice (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01)
      `the days the notice allows` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the notice sets for the graffiti to be obliterated
GIVETH A `Date answer`
`By what date must the graffiti be obliterated` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `by` (`s 18(2) -- the last day to comply with the notice` `the day the notice was given` `the days the notice allows`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export Has the local government's notice to obliterate the graffiti been complied with in time, or is there still time (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 18(2); counted on the notice's own dates, with no allowance for a suspension under s 24)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave the notice (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01)
      `the days the notice allows` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the notice sets for the graffiti to be obliterated
      `the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner the local government accepts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner acceptable to the local government (a rough cover-up it does not accept does not count)
      `the day it was obliterated` IS A DATE @desc The day the graffiti was obliterated (asked only if it has been)
      `today's date` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Done in time or not`
`Has the notice been complied with in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        -- Reading: graffiti obliterated before the notice was given leaves the notice nothing to require, so it is done
        -- in time. Settled here from the dates, not by the rule, which cannot judge an act before its start (finding 23).
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE IF `the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner the local government accepts` AND (`the day the notice was given` `is after` `the day it was obliterated`)
             THEN `done in time`
             ELSE `in time or not, from`
                      (`the outcome of` `the notice rule` `the notice recipient` (`ensure the graffiti is obliterated` `in a manner acceptable to the local government`) (`the day number of` `the day the notice was given`) `the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner the local government accepts` (`the day number of` `the day it was obliterated`) (`the day number of` `today's date`))
                      (`s 18(2) -- the last day to comply with the notice` `the day the notice was given` `the days the notice allows`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`
    WHERE
        -- A reasonable excuse changes what follows a failure (s 18(5)), not whether the graffiti went in time, so it is
        -- a fixed placeholder (FALSE) here; the fine question asks it.
        `the notice rule` MEANS `s 18(2), (5) -- the notice recipient must ensure the graffiti is obliterated within the time in the notice` `the days the notice allows` FALSE

@export If the notice is not complied with in time, what is the most I can be fined, with the further fine for each day or part of a day it goes on (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 18(5); counted on the notice's own dates, with no allowance for a suspension under s 24)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave the notice (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01)
      `the days the notice allows` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the notice sets for the graffiti to be obliterated
      `the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner the local government accepts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner acceptable to the local government (a rough cover-up it does not accept does not count)
      `the day it was obliterated` IS A DATE @desc The day the graffiti was obliterated (asked only if it has been)
      `today's date` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about (ISO 8601); the further fine is counted to this day while the graffiti remains
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court would accept that you had a reasonable excuse for not complying in time (for the court to decide; with one there is no offence)
GIVETH AN `Amount`
`What is the most I can be fined for not complying with the notice` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE IF NOT (`the notice was given to me` `the notice and me`)
             THEN amount 0
             ELSE CONSIDER `the outcome of` `the notice rule` `the notice recipient` (`ensure the graffiti is obliterated` `in a manner acceptable to the local government`) (`the day number of` `the day the notice was given`) `the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner the local government accepts` (`the day number of` `the day it was obliterated`) (`the day number of` `today's date`)
                  WHEN `the rule was not kept` THEN
                      IF `I had a reasonable excuse`
                      THEN amount 0
                      ELSE amount (`s 18(5) -- the maximum fine, with the further fine` (`s 18(5)(b) -- the days or part days after the last day to comply` `the day the notice was given` `the days the notice allows` `the day counted to`))
                  OTHERWISE amount 0
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`
    WHERE
        -- As in the question above: the excuse is asked here directly, so the rule's own excuse is a placeholder.
        `the notice rule` MEANS `s 18(2), (5) -- the notice recipient must ensure the graffiti is obliterated within the time in the notice` `the days the notice allows` FALSE
        `the day counted to` MEANS
            IF `the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner the local government accepts`
            THEN `the day it was obliterated`
            ELSE `today's date`

@export How much is the further fine for each day, or part of a day, that a failure to comply with the notice goes on (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 18(5)(b))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
GIVETH AN `Amount`
`How much is the further fine for each day the notice is not complied with` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE amount `s 18(5)(b) -- the further fine for each day or part of a day the offence continues`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export The notice was given to an occupier who is not the owner: must the owner be told in writing that it was given (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 18(3))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Must the owner be told in writing that the notice was given` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE CONSIDER `the person given the notice is` `the notice and me`
             WHEN JUST `how they stand` THEN `yes if` (`s 18(3) -- the owner is to be informed in writing that the notice was given` `how they stand`)
             -- Not reached: NOTHING is the no-notice case, which does not arise above.
             OTHERWISE no
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `s 19 -- if the notice is not complied with, and getting costs back`

@export If the notice is not complied with, may the local government obliterate the graffiti itself, and from whom can it recover its reasonable cost (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 19(3), (4); not while the notice is suspended, s 24)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner the local government accepts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner acceptable to the local government (a rough cover-up it does not accept does not count)
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave the notice (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01)
      `the days the notice allows` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the notice sets for the graffiti to be obliterated
      `today's date` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about (ISO 8601)
      `I have objected or applied for a review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or another affected person) have lodged an objection to the notice or applied to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review
      `it has been decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government or the Tribunal has decided how to dispose of the objection or application
      `the suspension has been revoked by order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person or Tribunal dealing with it has ordered that the suspension be revoked
      `the local government considers there are urgent reasons or a danger` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government considers there are urgent reasons not to suspend the notice, or that suspension is reasonably likely to endanger anyone's safety, damage property or create a serious public nuisance (its judgment; it must then give you written reasons, s 24(3))
GIVETH A `What the local government may do`
`What may the local government do if the notice is not complied with` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE IF `the graffiti has been obliterated in a manner the local government accepts`
             THEN `nothing: the graffiti has been obliterated`
             ELSE CONSIDER `the outcome of` (`s 18(2), (5) -- the notice recipient must ensure the graffiti is obliterated within the time in the notice` `the days the notice allows` FALSE) `the notice recipient` (`ensure the graffiti is obliterated` `in a manner acceptable to the local government`) (`the day number of` `the day the notice was given`) FALSE 0 (`the day number of` `today's date`)
                  WHEN `the rule is still open` THEN `nothing yet: the time in the notice has not run out`
                  OTHERWISE
                      -- s 24(1): while the notice is suspended it has no effect, so the s 19(3) power it brings waits too.
                      -- The s 24 facts are asked only once the time in the notice has run out.
                      IF `s 24(1) -- the effect of the notice is suspended` `I have objected or applied for a review` `it has been decided` `the suspension has been revoked by order` `the local government considers there are urgent reasons or a danger`
                      THEN `nothing for now: the notice is suspended until the objection or review is decided (s 24)`
                      ELSE CONSIDER `s 19(4) -- who the local government may recover its reasonable cost from` (`the notice was given to me` `the notice and me`) TRUE
                           WHEN `the person, who was given the notice and failed to comply with it` THEN `obliterate the graffiti, and recover its reasonable cost from you as a debt`
                           WHEN `the notice recipient, who is someone other than the person` THEN `obliterate the graffiti, and recover its reasonable cost from the person given the notice as a debt`
                           -- Not reached: the TRUE above is this branch's own fact, that the time ran out with the graffiti still there.
                           OTHERWISE `nothing: the graffiti has been obliterated`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export The notice cost me money: can a court order the owner (if I am the occupier) or the occupier (if I am the owner) to pay me back as much as it considers fair and reasonable (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 19(5)-(7))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `I had expense complying, or was fined, or paid the local government's cost` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you spent money complying with the notice, or did not comply and were fined or had to pay the local government's cost of obliterating the graffiti
GIVETH A `Who may be ordered to pay me back`
`Can I get some of what the notice cost me back from the owner or occupier` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE IF NOT (`the notice was given to me` `the notice and me`)
             THEN `no one: the notice was not given to you`
             ELSE CONSIDER `the person given the notice is` `the notice and me`
                  WHEN JUST `how I stand` THEN
                      CONSIDER `s 19(5), (6) -- who the court may order to pay the notice recipient` `how I stand` `I had expense complying, or was fined, or paid the local government's cost`
                      WHEN `the occupier` THEN `the occupier, as much as the court considers fair and reasonable`
                      WHEN `the owner` THEN `the owner, as much as the court considers fair and reasonable`
                      WHEN `no one, because the notice recipient is both the owner and the occupier` THEN `no one: you both own and occupy the property`
                      WHEN `no one, because the notice recipient has no expense, fine or cost of the kind in s 19(5)` THEN `no one: you have had no expense, fine or cost of the kind in s 19(5)`
                  OTHERWISE `no one: the notice was not given to you`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `ss 20-22 -- objecting to the notice`

@export Must I be given written reasons for the notice, and be told of my rights to object to it and to apply for a review (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) ss 20, 21)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Must I be given written reasons for the notice and told of my rights` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `yes if` (`s 21 -- the person is to be given written reasons and told of the rights to object and to apply for review` (`the s 20 position of` `the notice and me`))
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export May I object to the local government's notice now: am I an affected person who has not applied for a review, and is the time to object still open (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) ss 20, 22(1), (2); a last day on a weekend or public holiday moves to the next working day, but a holiday appointed by proclamation, such as the King's Birthday, is not counted)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `I have already applied to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review of the notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review of the decision to give the notice (then you cannot also object)
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave the notice (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01)
      `the further days the local government has allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days beyond the 28 the local government has allowed you to object (0 if none)
      `today's date` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`May I object to the notice` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE IF NOT (`s 22(1) -- the person may object to the notice` (`the s 20 position of` `the notice and me`) `I have already applied to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review of the notice`)
             THEN no
             ELSE IF `the day the notice was given` `is after` `today's date`
                  THEN no
                  ELSE CONSIDER `the outcome of` (`s 22(2) -- the affected person may object within 28 days after the notice is given, or any further time allowed` `the day the notice was given` `the further days the local government has allowed`) `the affected person` `lodge an objection in the approved form with the CEO of the local government` (`the day number of` `the day the notice was given`) FALSE 0 (`the day number of` `today's date`)
                       WHEN `the rule is still open` THEN yes
                       OTHERWISE no
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export By what date must I lodge my objection to the notice with the local government's chief executive officer, on its approved form (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 22(2); a last day on a weekend or public holiday moves to the next working day, Interpretation Act 1984 s 61(1)(e), but a holiday appointed by proclamation, such as the King's Birthday, is not counted)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave the notice (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01)
      `the further days the local government has allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days beyond the 28 the local government has allowed you to object (0 if none)
GIVETH A `Date answer`
`By what date must I lodge my objection` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `by` (`s 22(2) -- the last day to object` `the day the notice was given` `the further days the local government has allowed`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export Did I lodge my objection to the notice in time, or is there still time (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 22(2); a last day on a weekend or public holiday moves to the next working day, but a holiday appointed by proclamation, such as the King's Birthday, is not counted)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave the notice (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01)
      `the further days the local government has allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days beyond the 28 the local government has allowed you to object (0 if none)
      `I have lodged an objection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have lodged an objection, on the approved form, with the chief executive officer of the local government
      `the day I lodged my objection` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged your objection (asked only if you have)
      `today's date` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Done in time or not`
`Did I lodge my objection in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        -- An objection lodged before the notice was given objects to no notice; the rule cannot judge an act before its
        -- start (finding 23), so these two cases are settled from the dates.
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE IF `I have lodged an objection` AND (`the day the notice was given` `is after` `the day I lodged my objection`)
             THEN `done too early, before the right to do it arose on` `the day the notice was given`
             ELSE IF (NOT `I have lodged an objection`) AND (`the day the notice was given` `is after` `today's date`)
                  THEN `not open yet: it opens on` `the day the notice was given` `the last day to object`
                  ELSE `in time or not, from`
                           (`the outcome of` (`s 22(2) -- the affected person may object within 28 days after the notice is given, or any further time allowed` `the day the notice was given` `the further days the local government has allowed`) `the affected person` `lodge an objection in the approved form with the CEO of the local government` (`the day number of` `the day the notice was given`) `I have lodged an objection` (`the day number of` `the day I lodged my objection`) (`the day number of` `today's date`))
                           `the last day to object`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`
    WHERE
        `the last day to object` MEANS `s 22(2) -- the last day to object` `the day the notice was given` `the further days the local government has allowed`

@export My time to object has run out: can the local government still allow me further time to object (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 22(2); Interpretation Act 1984 s 64(1))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Can the local government still allow me further time to object` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    -- The s 22(2) rule's LEST: once the time runs out, the local government MAY allow further time. Fixed for the role,
    -- once there is a notice (D8).
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE yes
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `s 23 -- review by the State Administrative Tribunal`

@export May I apply to the State Administrative Tribunal now for a review of the notice, or of the decision on my objection: do I have the right, and is the time to apply still open (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) ss 20, 23(1)-(3); a last day on a weekend or public holiday moves to the next working day, but a holiday appointed by proclamation, such as the King's Birthday, is not counted)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave the notice (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01)
      `I have lodged an objection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have lodged an objection, on the approved form, with the chief executive officer of the local government
      `the day I lodged my objection` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged your objection (asked only if you have)
      `I have been given written notice of how my objection was decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government has given you notice in writing of how it decided to dispose of your objection
      `the day my objection was decided` IS A DATE @desc The day your objection was decided (asked only if you have been told the decision)
      `today's date` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`May I apply to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review now` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE IF NOT (`s 23(1), (2) -- the person may apply to the State Administrative Tribunal` `the right` (`the s 20 position of` `the notice and me`))
             THEN no
             ELSE IF `the first day to apply` `is after` `today's date`
                  THEN no
                  ELSE CONSIDER `the outcome of` (`s 23(3) -- the affected person may apply for review within 42 days after the right to apply arose` `the right` `the day the notice was given` `the day I lodged my objection` `the day my objection was decided`) `the affected person` `apply to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review` (`the day number of` (`s 23(3) -- the day the right to apply arose` `the right` `the day the notice was given` `the day I lodged my objection` `the day my objection was decided`)) FALSE 0 (`the day number of` `today's date`)
                       WHEN `the rule is still open` THEN yes
                       OTHERWISE no
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`
    WHERE
        `the right` MEANS `s 23(1), (2) -- the right to apply for review` `I have lodged an objection` `I have been given written notice of how my objection was decided` (`the days from` `the day I lodged my objection` `to` `today's date`)
        `the first day to apply` MEANS `s 23(3) -- the first day to apply` `the right` `the day the notice was given` `the day I lodged my objection` `the day my objection was decided`

@export By what date must I apply to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review, and from when (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 23(3): 42 days after the notice; more than 35 but within 77 days after an unanswered objection; 42 days after the objection was decided; a last day on a weekend or public holiday moves to the next working day, Interpretation Act 1984 s 61(1)(e), but a holiday appointed by proclamation, such as the King's Birthday, is not counted)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `I have lodged an objection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have lodged an objection, on the approved form, with the chief executive officer of the local government
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave the notice (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01)
      `the day I lodged my objection` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged your objection (asked only if you have)
      `I have been given written notice of how my objection was decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government has given you notice in writing of how it decided to dispose of your objection
      `the day my objection was decided` IS A DATE @desc The day your objection was decided (asked only if you have been told the decision)
      `today's date` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Date answer`
`By what date must I apply to the State Administrative Tribunal` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE CONSIDER `the right`
             WHEN `s 23(1)(b) -- an objection was lodged and no written decision was given within 35 days` THEN `between` `the first day to apply` `the last day to apply`
             WHEN `none yet -- an objection was lodged, is undecided, and 35 days have not yet passed` THEN `between` `the first day to apply` `the last day to apply`
             OTHERWISE `by` `the last day to apply`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`
    WHERE
        `the right` MEANS `s 23(1), (2) -- the right to apply for review` `I have lodged an objection` `I have been given written notice of how my objection was decided` (`the days from` `the day I lodged my objection` `to` `today's date`)
        `the first day to apply` MEANS `s 23(3) -- the first day to apply` `the right` `the day the notice was given` `the day I lodged my objection` `the day my objection was decided`
        `the last day to apply` MEANS `s 23(3) -- the last day to apply` `the right` `the day the notice was given` `the day I lodged my objection` `the day my objection was decided`

@export Did I apply to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review in time, or is there still time (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 23(3); a last day on a weekend or public holiday moves to the next working day, but a holiday appointed by proclamation, such as the King's Birthday, is not counted)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `I have lodged an objection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have lodged an objection, on the approved form, with the chief executive officer of the local government
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave the notice (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-01)
      `the day I lodged my objection` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged your objection (asked only if you have)
      `I have been given written notice of how my objection was decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government has given you notice in writing of how it decided to dispose of your objection
      `the day my objection was decided` IS A DATE @desc The day your objection was decided (asked only if you have been told the decision)
      `the day I was given written notice of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the local government gave you that written notice (asked only if it has); an application made before then was made under s 23(1)(b), not s 23(2)
      `I have applied to the State Administrative Tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review
      `the day I applied` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied to the Tribunal (asked only if you have)
      `today's date` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Done in time or not`
`Did I apply to the State Administrative Tribunal in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE IF `I have applied to the State Administrative Tribunal` AND (`the first day to apply` `is after` `the day I applied`)
             THEN `done too early, before the right to do it arose on` `the first day to apply`
             ELSE IF (NOT `I have applied to the State Administrative Tribunal`) AND (`the first day to apply` `is after` `today's date`)
                  THEN `not open yet: it opens on` `the first day to apply` `the last day to apply`
                  ELSE `in time or not, from`
                           (`the outcome of` (`s 23(3) -- the affected person may apply for review within 42 days after the right to apply arose` `the right` `the day the notice was given` `the day I lodged my objection` `the day my objection was decided`) `the affected person` `apply to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review` (`the day number of` (`s 23(3) -- the day the right to apply arose` `the right` `the day the notice was given` `the day I lodged my objection` `the day my objection was decided`)) `I have applied to the State Administrative Tribunal` (`the day number of` `the day I applied`) (`the day number of` `today's date`))
                           `the last day to apply`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`
    WHERE
        -- The route is worked out on the day asked about, or on the day of the application if there was one: the
        -- written notice of the decision counts only if it had been given by that day, so a notice that comes after an
        -- application under s 23(1)(b) does not turn it into an early application under s 23(2).
        `the right` MEANS `s 23(1), (2) -- the right to apply for review` `I have lodged an objection` `written notice of the decision had been given by then` (`the days from` `the day I lodged my objection` `to` `the day the route is judged on`)
        `written notice of the decision had been given by then` MEANS
                `I have been given written notice of how my objection was decided`
            AND (NOT (`the day I was given written notice of the decision` `is after` `the day the route is judged on`))
        `the day the route is judged on` MEANS
            IF `I have applied to the State Administrative Tribunal` THEN `the day I applied` ELSE `today's date`
        `the first day to apply` MEANS `s 23(3) -- the first day to apply` `the right` `the day the notice was given` `the day I lodged my objection` `the day my objection was decided`
        `the last day to apply` MEANS `s 23(3) -- the last day to apply` `the right` `the day the notice was given` `the day I lodged my objection` `the day my objection was decided`

@export My time to apply to the State Administrative Tribunal has run out: can I ask the Tribunal to extend it (State Administrative Tribunal Act 2004 s 92, where the Tribunal's rules allow and no one is unfairly prejudiced)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Can I ask the Tribunal to extend my time to apply` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    -- The s 23(3) rule's LEST: once the time runs out, the affected person MAY ask for an extension. Fixed for the role,
    -- once there is a notice (D8).
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE yes
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `s 24 -- suspension of the notice`

-- Arises only once a notice has been given (after consent) and someone has objected or applied for a review: the
-- objection fact comes straight after the notice (D8).
@export Is the notice suspended, so that it has no effect, while my objection or application for review is being decided (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 24)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `the notice and me` IS A `The notice and me` @desc Who the local government gave a written notice under s 18 to (you, the owner or the occupier), and whether you own the property, occupy it, or both
      `I have objected or applied for a review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or another affected person) have lodged an objection to the notice or applied to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review
      `it has been decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government or the Tribunal has decided how to dispose of the objection or application
      `the suspension has been revoked by order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person or Tribunal dealing with it has ordered that the suspension be revoked
      `the local government considers there are urgent reasons or a danger` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government considers there are urgent reasons not to suspend the notice, or that suspension is reasonably likely to endanger anyone's safety, damage property or create a serious public nuisance (its judgment; it must then give you written reasons, s 24(3))
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is the notice suspended while my objection or review is decided` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the notice situation does not hold` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the notice and me`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE IF NOT (`I have objected or applied for a review`)
             THEN `does not arise for you`
             ELSE `yes if` (`s 24(1) -- the effect of the notice is suspended` `I have objected or applied for a review` `it has been decided` `the suspension has been revoked by order` `the local government considers there are urgent reasons or a danger`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `ss 27-30 -- the local government entering your land`

-- Each question here arises only where the local government wants to come on to the land: that fact comes second, and
-- without it the answer is `does not arise for you` (D8).
@export May the local government, or someone acting for it, lawfully enter my land: with my consent, a justice's warrant, or a notice given at least 24 hours before and not objected to (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) ss 27, 28, 29)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the local government wants to come on to my land` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government, or someone it authorised, wants to enter your land under Part 3 (to obliterate graffiti, or to see whether a notice has been complied with)
      `the owner or occupier consented to the entry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, or the other owner or occupier, consented to the entry
      `a justice's warrant authorises the entry and is still in effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a justice has issued a warrant for the entry that is still in effect (a warrant lasts until its purpose is met or one month has passed, whichever is shorter, s 29(3))
      `notice of the entry was given to the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government gave the owner or occupier a notice of the intended entry, stating its purpose (s 28)
      `how many hours before the entry the notice was given` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours passed between the notice of entry being given and the entry (or the intended entry), such as 30 or 23.5 (asked only if a notice was given)
      `the owner or occupier, or someone they authorised, objects to the entry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, the other owner or occupier, or someone authorised by one of you, objects to the entry
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`May the local government enter my land` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF NOT (`the local government wants to come on to my land`)
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE CONSIDER `the outcome of` (`ss 27, 28 -- the local government may enter only with consent, a warrant, or at least 24 hours' notice` `the owner or occupier consented to the entry` `a justice's warrant authorises the entry and is still in effect` `notice of the entry was given to the owner or occupier` `the owner or occupier, or someone they authorised, objects to the entry`) `the local government` `enter the land` 0 TRUE `the minute of the entry` 0
             WHEN `the rule was kept` THEN yes
             OTHERWISE no
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`
    WHERE
        -- The entry rule counts whole minutes from the notice; a part minute short of 24 hours is still short.
        `the minute of the entry` MEANS
            IF `notice of the entry was given to the owner or occupier`
            THEN TRUNC (`how many hours before the entry the notice was given` TIMES 60) 0
            ELSE 0

@export If the local government relies on a notice to enter my land without my consent, how long before the entry must the notice be given (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 28(3))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the local government wants to come on to my land` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government, or someone it authorised, wants to enter your land under Part 3 (to obliterate graffiti, or to see whether a notice has been complied with)
GIVETH A `Hours`
`How much notice of entry must I be given` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF NOT (`the local government wants to come on to my land`)
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `at least` `s 28(3) -- the hours of notice to be given before entry`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

@export If I ask, must the person entering my land for the local government tell me the power under which it claims the right to enter (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 30)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the local government wants to come on to my land` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government, or someone it authorised, wants to enter your land under Part 3 (to obliterate graffiti, or to see whether a notice has been complied with)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Must the person entering tell me the power under which they enter` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    -- Asked on the footing that you ask, so the s 30 fact is TRUE by the question's own terms.
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF NOT (`the local government wants to come on to my land`)
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `yes if` (`s 30 -- the person entering is to give particulars of the power of entry` TRUE)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `s 25 and s 5 -- graffiti put on your property without your consent`

-- Both questions arise only for graffiti put up without the consent of the owner or occupier (graffiti with it is a
-- s 18 matter): the consent fact comes second, and where it is TRUE the answer is `does not arise for you` (D8).
@export Someone put graffiti on my property without consent: may the local government remove it, even though it is not the council's land and I have not agreed (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 25; entering still needs ss 27-29)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `it is visible from a public place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it can be seen from a public place: anywhere the public may go, a school or other place of education (except parts closed to students and the public), or an unoccupied private place (s 14)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`May the local government remove graffiti put on my property without consent` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `yes if` (`s 25(1) -- the local government may obliterate the graffiti without consent` `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` `it is visible from a public place`)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

-- In this situation neither the owner nor the occupier consented, and "my property" is the owner's or the occupier's,
-- so the s 5(1) consent of the person the property belongs to is FALSE by the situation's own terms (D8; NOTES.md). The
-- property is another person's from the point of view of whoever put the mark on it: TRUE by the question's terms.
@export Someone else put this mark on my property: was that an offence by them (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) ss 3, 5)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or the occupier of the property (you, or the other), such as a commissioned mural or a tag you allowed
      `how the mark was applied` IS A `How the mark was applied` @desc How the mark was put on the property: by spraying, writing, drawing, marking or otherwise applying paint or another marking substance; by scratching or etching; or in some other way, such as sticking on a poster or sticker (not graffiti under s 3)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Was it an offence to put this mark on my property` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF `the graffiti was put there with the consent of the owner or occupier`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `yes if it was broken` (`the outcome of` (`s 5(1) -- a person must not damage another person's property by graffiti without consent` `how the mark was applied` TRUE FALSE) `the person` `put the mark on the property` 0 TRUE 0 0)
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`

§§§ `s 12 -- someone hurt removing graffiti on your property`

@export Can I be sued if someone is hurt or killed while removing graffiti on my property under the Act or a community order (Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) s 12, which protects only the owner of the place)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `graffiti removal work has been or will be done on my property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether graffiti removal work is done on your property under the Act, or by someone doing community work under a court order
      `I am an owner of the place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an owner of the place in the sense of the Local Government Act 1995 s 1.4 (through s 3): in the main the freeholder in possession or a purchaser with a right to the freehold, the owner of a strata leasehold lot, a Crown lessee or a lessee of land rateable only in the lessee's hands, a mortgagee, or a trustee or agent of one of them. An ordinary tenant renting from a private landlord is not an owner
      `a breach of my duty of care would amount to gross negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court would find that your breach of your duty of care to the person amounted to gross negligence (for the court to decide; otherwise the action lies against the State instead)
GIVETH A `Whether I can be sued`
`Can I be sued if someone is hurt removing graffiti on my property` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I own or occupy property with graffiti on it` THEN
        IF NOT (`graffiti removal work has been or will be done on my property`)
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE CONSIDER `s 12(1)-(3) -- whether an action in tort lies against the person` `I am an owner of the place` `a breach of my duty of care would amount to gross negligence`
             WHEN `the action does not lie against the owner, and may be taken against the State instead` THEN `no: as the owner, an action in tort does not lie against you; it may be taken against the State instead`
             WHEN `the action lies against the owner, because the breach amounts to gross negligence` THEN `yes: your breach of your duty of care would amount to gross negligence, which s 12 does not protect`
             WHEN `s 12 does not apply, because the person is not the owner of the place` THEN `s 12 does not protect you: it protects only the owner of the place, not an occupier who is not its owner`
    OTHERWISE `does not arise for you`
